[
  {
    "title": "The Blood of the River",
    "body": "Dylan Saccoccio frames the episode around Reverend Robert Taylor, jailed for blasphemy, who argued that Western literalism mistook Eastern figures of speech for history, so that wine called the blood of the grape and the Nile called the blood of the river both hardened into doctrine. Because the river was Egypt's god, those washed in it were spoken of as washed in blood, and the annual inundation became a bloodshedding for the life of the world. Chance Garton adds that the father of ecclesiastical history described the Therapeutae, the priesthood of Alexandria, as already holding the gospels almost word for word before Jesus."
  },
  {
    "title": "Fire and Water Made One",
    "body": "Both hosts treat sun and water as two faces of a single animating force rather than rival symbols. Chance Garton argues that without water the sun would burn everything up, and without the sun water would sit inert, frozen and unmoving, so one figure can be the Nile and the solar principle at once. Dylan Saccoccio notes the inundation beginning near the summer solstice in the month of Thoth, running blood red with sediment before the river was dammed, and he flags Taylor's claim that the blood of the lamb encodes an inundation under Aries as something they cannot prove."
  },
  {
    "title": "The Dog Star and Caleb",
    "body": "Following Diodorus Siculus, Dylan Saccoccio traces the Nile's most ancient name to Okianus, also written Ogenos, the perfect river of Hesiod and the stream in which Homer's stars bathe, then reads Taylor's parsing of Osiris as the star and the fire. Sir Drummond is cited for the observation that Sirius rose helically as the flood approached its greatest height, which is why the Indian Bacchus is drawn always accompanied by a dog. Moses wanders with Caleb, literally Hebrew for dog, who stilled the people in Numbers 13:30, and the star's forty days below the horizon shadow the wilderness wandering."
  },
  {
    "title": "Wolf Skins and Priestly Bloodlines",
    "body": "The dog thread runs straight into priesthood. Kuon, the dog, is offered as a cognate of Cohen, with dog headed cynocephali posted at the equinoxes, and Chance Garton points to the Etruscan father god shown in a wolf helmet whose priests wore wolf skins and wolf belts. Dylan Saccoccio quotes Leonard Cohen telling the Village Voice in 1967 that he had a messianic childhood, told he descended from Aaron the high priest. Taylor's text makes Aaron the first of priests, the saint of the Lord in the 106th Psalm, anointed head, beard and skirts, and so the Christ, the besmeared."
  },
  {
    "title": "The Golden Calf Today",
    "body": "Taylor mocks the story in which Aaron, after hearing God at Sinai and working the plagues in Egypt, abandons it all the moment his brother's back is turned and builds the golden calf. Chance Garton reads the incestuous line of Amram and Jochebed as forensic evidence of mythology, since only pantheons can inbreed without consequence, and notes that Elisheba matches Elizabeth, wife of Zacharias. Dylan Saccoccio then ties the calf to Iranian effigies of Baal, which he reads as bowl and therefore Super Bowl as above the Lord, to the season of sacrifice, and to crows flocking over Tel Aviv."
  }
]

